:"gate","H7378":"to contend","H7379":"strife","H6906":"to rob","H853":"[Obj.]","H7462":"House of Shepherds","H854":"with","H1167":"master","H639":"anger","H2534":"rage","H935":"come","H6435":"lest","H502":"to teach/learn","H734":"way","H3947":"take","H4170":"snare","H8628":"to blow","H3709":"palm","H6148":"to pledge","H4859":"loan","H518":"if","H369":"nothing","H7999":"to complete","H4100":"what?","H4904":"bed","H8478":"under","H5253":"to remove","H1366":"boundary","H5769":"enduring","H834":"which","H1":"father","H2372":"to see","H4106":"quick","H4399":"work","H6440":"before","H3320":"to stand","H1077":"not","H2823":"obscure"},"ccc":{},"cccp":{},"com":[["haydock",1,null,"haydock","Good. Hebrew, “a name,” or reputation. (Haydock) — It is preferable to riches, but not to be compared with virtue, which is the only solid good; and even to be placed above riches, it must be well grounded. (Calmet) — Favour with all. (Haydock)"],["haydock",2,null,"haydock","Another. They stand in need of one another. (St. Chrysostom, hom. xxxiv. in 1 Corinthians.) — They are equal in God’s sight, who only values real virtue. He disposes of riches, so that the poor may one day become rich. (Calmet)"],["haydock",4,null,"haydock","Fruit. Literally, “the end of modesty, (Haydock) or moderation, which must accompany every virtue."],["haydock",5,null,"haydock","Perverse. They are always in danger and in trouble."],["haydock",6,null,"haydock","It is a proverb, is added by St. Jerome, to make the sentence more striking. — It. He is like a tender plant, (Calmet) or wax, or a new vessel."],["haydock",7,null,"haydock","Servant. He might be sold, &c., Exodus xxii. 3., and Matthew xviii. 25. (Gell. xx. 1.) Plato (Leg. viii.) would have nothing sold on credit. These laws appear to be severe; but they are founded on wisdom, as nothing impoverishes more than the facility of borrowing."],["haydock",8,null,"haydock","Consumed. Or beaten with the flail of God’s anger."],["haydock",9,null,"haydock","Is. Hebrew, “has a good eye,” in opposition to the evil, or malicious one, Matthew xx. 15. — He, &c., is not in Hebrew, or in the Latin edition of Comp. and St. Jerome."],["haydock",11,null,"haydock","He. Septuagint, “the Lord loveth pious hearts. All the irreproachable are acceptable to him. The king feeds with lips,” by his just ordinances. (Haydock) — Kings hate duplicity; but require that the truth should be disclosed to them in a suitable manner."],["haydock",13,null,"haydock","Streets. Vain excuses of sloth!"],["haydock",14,null,"haydock","It. Debauchery resembles hell, chap. xxiii. 23. (Calmet) — Facilis descensus Averni, &c. (Virgil, Æneid vi.)"],["haydock",15,null,"haydock","Folly. Ignorance and innate corruption are corrected by a good education."],["haydock",17,null,"haydock","Incline. Thus Solomon concludes his discourse, (chap. xxiv. 23.) in the same manner as he began it, to chap. x. Some commence the third book of Proverbs in this place; others, chap. xxv. (Calmet)"],["haydock",18,null,"haydock","Lips. Out of the abundance of the heart the mouth speaketh. (Haydock)"],["haydock",20,null,"haydock","Ways. Repeatedly. (Bossuet; Tirinus) (2 Corinthians xii. 8., and Amos i. 11.) (Calmet) — Protestants, “have not I written to thee excellent things in counsels and knowledge?” “Shalishim,” perfect, (Pagnin) or “three things,” (Montanus) means also (Haydock) such as might suit princes and great officers. (Calmet)"],["haydock",21,null,"haydock","Sent. Septuagint, “are sent to thee.” Thou mayst become a teacher, (Haydock) or give satisfaction to thy parents, who have sent thee to my school. (Calmet)"],["haydock",22,null,"haydock","Gate. Where judges passed sentence. (Menochius)"],["haydock",25,null,"haydock","Soul. By imitating him, or by falling a victim to his rage."],["haydock",26,null,"haydock","Hands. Engaging to stand bond. (Haydock) (Chap. vi. 1.) — Such a one might be required to pay the debt, chap. xx. 16."],["haydock",28,null,"haydock","Set. The pagans made a god of Terminus, to prevent disputes. (Ovid, Fast. ii.) — If it be unlawful to disturb land-marks, how much more so is it to give way to novelty in religion? (Deuteronomy xix. 14.) (Calmet) — Solomon is addressing those who follow the true faith. Else the conduct of infidel ancestors should not deter any from embracing the truth. (Haydock)"],["haydock",29,null,"haydock","Obscure. By industry he shall raise himself to notice. (Haydock) — Kings employ those who are most active. (Calmet)"]],"au":{"haydock":["George Leo Haydock",0,0,1849,"https://www.newadvent.org/cathen/07160a.htm"]}}